t it i guess  well i think there's been a lot of good  tics uh as beth rigby said there in your  report in the special relationship it's  back on track maybe after a wobble a  concern about confidence in it post the  speedy afghan and withdrawal it might  have been a great bonus that will have  more development on this trade deal and  certainly it seems that the air has gone  out of it uh with donald trump um being  replaced by joe biden the democratic  president and issues there he's  concerned about northern ireland and and  he made made quite clear in response to  the beth's question at that that um that  huddle there in in the white house so i  think you know it would have been nice  to have got there have been side deals  done on scotch whiskey imports no  tariffs on that since the summer last  september there was deals on scotch beef  but there's no question that there's a  lot that's not done um brexit you know  is a ten-year project probably to get  right  a big shame i think for the pm it looks  like he's saying that interviews he will  not get any deal done by 2024 the likely  of next election um so the idea of a  brexit dividend is not going to happen  uh at least not immediately with the us  but certainly in terms of relationships  and after that deal there on on  intelligence with australia um  uh and uh and uh others you know i think  that there's a lot to say that with the  u.s should say that there's a lot to say  there's a lot for the special  relationship more than seem to be there  in august yeah similar theme for the  guardian sonia um you know everybody's  quoting boris johnson telling our  political editor beth rigby that well  you know they negotiate very hard uh the  americans we're going as fast as we can  uh we heard also joe biden there saying  it's going to be discussed they'll go on  for a private meeting after that spray  as they call it with the journalists  there some invited journalists in but if  we move on to the daily telegraph  um the suggestion that the uk may join  the u.s mexico canada trade club as  hopes fade for a loan deal with america  this falling to the new foreign  secretary liz truss who's already raised  stalled negotiations with u.s secretary  of state anthony blinken when they met  in new york during the u.n general  assembly um the pa news agency  suggesting that miss trust questioned  whether that position is tenable in the  long term and has considered other  routes to improving trading ties with  the united states so i mean you know it  feels a sort of backdoor way of getting  somewhere um with these nations of north  america what do you reckon  well i think it's very  optimal from the prime minister's uh  perspective uh a trade deal with the us  was supposed to be the big brexit prize  it was the key plank of the prime  minister's vision uh for post-brexit  britain and our trading future and i  think this is essentially that  post-brexit vision colliding with  reality  lots of trade experts said at the time  that the prime minister and vote leave  were far too bombastic about the  prospect of a bilateral trade deal with  the us the uk is a pretty small economy  um standalone as things go the us has  got um bigger fish to fry as i think um  you know  the prime minister said  and um so it was always going to be  extremely unlikely that we were going to  be sort of um way ahead in the queue for  a trade deal particularly with president  trump out of the white house so i do  think that you know that the talk has  moved to the prospect of the uk trying  to join um an agreement that already  exists  between the nations of north america the  us canada and mexico it is definitely  suboptimal i think at the end of the day  when we take a step back from this it  does sort of raise questions about what  we were trying to achieve  uh with brexit because obviously one  post-brexit option available to us was  to sort of stay part of the single  market the eu but to leave the political  institutions so to stay economically  aligned and the truth of it is is that  that vote leave told us that this was  about taking back control but you can  never strike a trade deal with other  countries particularly when you're  negotiating with a block far larger than  yourself and i come back to the fact  that the uk by itself is a modest sized  economy compared to these big trading  books that we're talking about you can  never negotiate a trade deal and not  give up some sovereignty in terms of  regulatory alignment that is the price  of a trade deal so the the question  facing close brexit britain was always  do you want to align in a regulatory  nature with the eu or do you want to  align with the us and there's all sorts  of issues around food standards in the  us for example chlorinated chicken is  just a sort of thin end of the wedge  which means that i think actually the uk  public isn't going to be that happy when  it finds out what's signing up to a  trade deal  with the u.s mexico and canada really  means you know of our values when it  comes to the labor market food standards  etc are actually they lie much more with  our neighbours on the continent  and um just very quickly christopher uh  we saw those pictures of kamala harris  and the  the prime minister you know sharing a  look at the view um will he go away  really satisfied by this do you think  given the ticks we've talked about the  extra commitment to the climate change  budget for developing nations  this lifting of the of the travel ban on  brits going to america is that enough do  you think  it's certainly something as a former uh  american uh national until 2016 when he  announced it boris johnson will have  will have felt that quite quite quite  importantly you know why can't we have  this uh this steady flow of people to  and fro across the atlantic for me it is  kind of continuity obama uh obama said  didn't he back in 2016 would be at the  back of the q fred trade deal it looks  like you know joe biden is his vice  president is saying saying as much now  now he's in charge of the white house i  think there's i think that they've done  the best they could i think it's been  difficult uh since the afghan withdrawal  but i think they have got that you know  they're moving towards uh  you know a way of working i i do share  someone's concern about a deal with the  us mexico and canada i don't think the  choice is offering was a fair one it's a  false choice to say we sign up to all of  the all of what all of the uh different  levels of standards in all that in those  three countries not quite the same as  that but i think um yeah i think uh it's  more to work on  um food shortage fears averted sonja um  what's the deal  so the government um it's emerging  tonight has struck a deal he would have  thought that gas shortages would have  had such a big knock on him on the uk's  food supply chain but it does because  co2 is used in so many forms of food  production and food transport it emerges  tonight that the government has struck a  very short term deal with one of the  major co2 suppliers cf fertilizers  financial support for three weeks to try  and get co2 production back on track but  i don't think that that's going to  answer the longer term questions about  the sustainability of co2 production  given the gas shortages are forecast to  sort of last a lot longer than just the  next couple of weeks absolutely uh co2  and all the things it does who knew but  the fundamentals are the gas prices are  still going up and effectively  householders and these failing  businesses now as we see will will meet  the costs of that christopher  and so will taxpayers we don't know  where the full at the full cost of the  deal signed by quasi-quattang tonight  it'll be millions and we're all paying  for it don't forget we have got this  issue of the review of the of the energy  price cap of the first of october and  then in april next year council tax  bills going up the national insurance  increase the special wages caused by  that we're in for a very difficult cost  of living a few months i think and that  that will define i think the period  going into the christmas it should be a  lot harder more expensive just to live  in this country not easy yeah absolutely  inflationary pressures globally not just  here that was the warning today wasn't  it thank you both very much indeed

